  "evidences": [
    {
      "id": "ev_2",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s2"
      },
      "text": "During this investigation we could identify the threat actor behind these attacks as LazyScripter, an emerging APT group pointed by MalwareBytes in February 2021.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_4",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s4"
      },
      "text": "Additionally, after the analysis of the samples, we discovered the usage of a free and popular online obfuscating tool for scripts, which would inject their own downloader for a njRAT sample within LazyScripter’s malware.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_8",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p2_s1"
      },
      "text": "PDF document from spear phishing After the analysis of the first pdf document that ended up in our hands (“JOB NOTICE.pdf” – UNWTO) we did not observed embedded code, or any malicious behavior.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_17",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s2"
      },
      "text": "LIST OF AVAILABLE JOBS.js SALARY AND HIRING CONDITIONS.js This highly obfuscated JavaScript has the only purpose of dropping a second VBS script, which will be placed in the following paths:",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_18",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s3"
      },
      "text": "C:\\Users\\*\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Start Menu\\Programs\\Startup\\tk.vbs C:\\Users\\*\\AppData\\Roaming\\tk.vbs For those samples where the VBS script was not dropped in the startup folder, the following persistence mechanism would be established using the registry keys:",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_20",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 5,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p5_s2"
      },
      "text": "At this point we could find an attribution according to different reports, since the domain stub.]ignorelist.]com had been used by the group referred as LazyScripter in their previous campaign.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_25",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 6,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p6_s2"
      },
      "text": "After deobfuscating the VBS script we could identify the malware sample as Houdini’s H- Worm , but preceded by an interesting line, still slightly obfuscated.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_30",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 7,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p7_s2"
      },
      "text": "This script will be the one which implements the RAT identified as H-worm after a complex nested obfuscation, prepended with a confusing extra line.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_35",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 9,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p9_s1"
      },
      "text": "tk.vbs deobfuscated Now, we could know that this VBS script acted as some sort of loader for the final stage artifact, which was fully implemented in the aforementioned last line, supposed to be a commented line in VBS.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_36",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 10,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p10_s1"
      },
      "text": "Before analyzing this extra suspicious code, which we could corroborate it was not part of the known source code for H-Worm, the obvious thought was that these lines were added by the LazyScripter criminals and that they were placing dates in the script for their own reasons.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_37",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 10,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p10_s2"
      },
      "text": "After the analysis of the snippets, we observed that the samples would compare the current date with the hardcoded date, and if the hardcoded day arrived or passed, it would execute a specific function appended at the end of H-Worm’s code.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_38",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 10,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p10_s3"
      },
      "text": "This function would only drop the previously described .lnk file and set the mentioned registry key values so as to download a sample of njRAT.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_39",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 10,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p10_s4"
      },
      "text": "Even though the author of H-Worm, known as “Houdini” had been connected to the development of njRAT, we knew this wasn’t part of the known implementation for H-Worm, and still looked odd as a TTP from the same infection campaign.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_40",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 10,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p10_s5"
      },
      "text": "Trying to make sense out of it, we had the brainwave of using the information we had about this parallel behavior and make a quick check: We previously found out that they might have been using hackfree.]org as an online obfuscation service for VBS script, so we created our own dummy VBS script and submitted it to hackfree for obfuscation.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_44",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Lab52_Lazyscripters-double-compromise-single-obfuscation(03-09-2022)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 12,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p12_s2"
      },
      "text": "Finally, back to the tracked threat actor, we could distinguish between LazyScripter’s indicators of compromise, and HackFree’s IOCs, resulting in the following diagram for this LazyScripter campaign main infrastructure and infection chain.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
